# Add Varlock environment contracts

## Problem

A Vercel production env pull returned short, identical placeholder values for `INNGEST_EVENT_KEY` and `INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY`. AI Hero production remained healthy because the live deployment had effective Inngest credentials from the Vercel integration, but local refund and inspection commands mistook the redacted placeholders for broken production credentials.

Presence-only validation did not distinguish real credentials from placeholders. The operator path then misdiagnosed a production outage.

## Decision

Add Varlock as a validation layer before replacing the existing T3/Zod runtime loader.

The first contract covers only the two Inngest credentials:

- values remain sensitive and are required only in production
- both keys must be at least 32 characters
- signing keys must use an Inngest production or test prefix
- `prebuild` validates the effective ambient build credentials before `next build`
- `pnpm env:check` provides the same redacted local check

Do not validate integration-managed secrets from a Vercel project env pull. Vercel returns bracketed sensitive placeholders rather than the effective deployment values. The Vercel build environment is the authoritative validation point.

## Scope boundary

This change does not replace `src/env.mjs`, inject resolved values into Next.js, rotate credentials, mutate Vercel, or change runtime environment loading.

Expand the schema after launch. Migrate one provider boundary at a time and keep the existing runtime validator until the Varlock contract is complete.

## Verification

- `pnpm env:check` passes when the keys are absent locally or in preview
- simulated Vercel production fails when either key is absent
- a synthetic valid pair passes Varlock validation
- synthetic short keys fail with redacted validation errors
- `pnpm typecheck` passes
- full lint remains blocked by pre-existing React errors; the changed configuration and schema add no lint findings
